{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And if a man strikes, etc.</b> In Kiddushin (Kiddushin 24b) Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel and the Rabbis disagree: Rabban Shimon ben Gamliel holds that he must intend to destroy it, while the Rabbis hold that even if he did not intend to destroy it, he must nevertheless intend [to strike] the eye itself. And it is difficult according to the Rabbis: the verse should have said ״וְכִי יַשְׁחִית אִישׁ אֶת עֵין עַבְדּוֹ״. And perhaps I would say that the destruction which the verse mentions is meant literally for its own sake, and we would not have known that the verse intends to state this as a condition — that he must intend it." "<b>Further,</b> it appears that it intends to say that even if he did not destroy it at the very moment of the blow itself but only after some time, the law would still be so. And it is not comparable to the case of unintentional [killing] concerning which we wrote above that he must die at that very moment. And the Rabbis' derivation that he must intend [to strike] the eye is heard from the designation ״וְשִׁחֲתָהּ״:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:02.112184822Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}